WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:18.101
your mother, Jack," I said as we stood at the mouth of the dark cave.

2
00:00:18.942 --> 00:00:20.683
"And bring as many candles as you can.

3
00:00:21.124 --> 00:00:23.165
We'll need some light to explore this place."

4
00:00:24.666 --> 00:00:27.848
Jack jumped into the cart and urged Storm, the buffalo, to hurry.

5
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:33.152
He soon returned with my wife, Franz, and the two dogs.

6
00:00:34.204 --> 00:00:36.226
Everyone was excited about our discovery.

7
00:00:36.246 --> 00:00:38.828
"Are we really going to live here?"

8
00:00:39.188 --> 00:00:39.848
asked Franz.

9
00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:41.450
"Where will I sleep?"

10
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.611
"How big is this cave?"

11
00:00:43.912 --> 00:00:44.532
asked my wife.

12
00:00:44.552 --> 00:00:47.855
"Let's light some candles and find out," I replied.

13
00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:51.978
With our candles lit, we entered the cave and everyone fell silent.

14
00:00:52.738 --> 00:00:54.780
The vast cave was like a crystal palace.

15
00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:00.024
The candles cast a golden light on the walls, which sparkled like stars.

16
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.543
Great crystal pillars rose from the floor and hung from the ceiling.

17
00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:06.444
"What are these crystals?"

18
00:01:06.824 --> 00:01:07.485
asked Ernest.

19
00:01:08.265 --> 00:01:09.486
"Rock salt," I said.

20
00:01:09.506 --> 00:01:10.927
"And look at the floor.

21
00:01:11.487 --> 00:01:13.329
It's made of hard, dry sand.

22
00:01:14.089 --> 00:01:17.612
We can live here without the slightest fear of getting wet during the rainy season."

23
00:01:18.312 --> 00:01:21.735
We returned to Falconhurst with our minds full of wonder at our discovery.

24
00:01:22.855 --> 00:01:24.557
All we talked about was our new house.

25
00:01:25.497 --> 00:01:29.952
Our nest in the tree had seemed so safe and comfortable compared to Tentholm.

26
00:01:29.972 --> 00:01:33.214
But now we decided to keep Falconhurst only as a summer residence.

27
00:01:34.155 --> 00:01:37.257
So we set to work on the cave, which we named Rockburg.

28
00:01:38.058 --> 00:01:40.079
The first thing was to provide light and air.

29
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.303
We cut openings in the rock, and fit them with a door and some windows we'd brought from

30
00:01:45.343 --> 00:01:45.683
the ship.

31
00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:48.505
Then we divided the cave into rooms.

32
00:01:49.506 --> 00:01:53.229
Our living space included areas for sitting, eating, and sleeping.

33
00:01:54.129 --> 00:01:56.031
To the right were our kitchen and workshop.

34
00:01:56.451 --> 00:01:57.732
To the left were the stables.

35
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.341
Behind all this, in the dark recess of the cave, was our storage area.

36
00:02:04.302 --> 00:02:08.803
We had learned from the last rainy season that we needed to have a proper fireplace and chimney.

37
00:02:09.663 --> 00:02:12.204
Our stable arrangements were much better this time too.

38
00:02:13.004 --> 00:02:15.845
And there was plenty of space in our workshop for the biggest project.

39
00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:20.846
During this time we made frequent trips to Tentholm.

40
00:02:21.627 --> 00:02:25.848
The shore provided us with plenty to eat: lobsters, crabs, and mussels.

41
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.341
But one morning there was an additional surprise for us.

42
00:02:30.202 --> 00:02:34.964
The sea rolled and heaved, and rays from the sun lit up the water as if it were

43
00:02:35.024 --> 00:02:35.524
on fire.

44
00:02:35.544 --> 00:02:37.965
"The sea is boiling!"

45
00:02:38.325 --> 00:02:39.126
exclaimed Jack.

46
00:02:39.146 --> 00:02:42.367
"Why are there flashes of silver in the water?"

47
00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:43.447
asked Ernest.

48
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:45.788
"And what are those birds doing?"

49
00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:46.729
said Fritz.

50
00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:50.971
Hovering over the water, hundreds of birds were screaming loudly.

51
00:02:51.711 --> 00:02:53.312
Then they dove toward the water.

52
00:02:54.164 --> 00:02:59.246
Meanwhile a shining, sparkling silver mass in the water rolled directly toward our shore.

53
00:02:59.266 --> 00:03:02.068
"That's a shoal of herring," I said.

54
00:03:02.868 --> 00:03:04.769
"Millions of these fish swim together.

55
00:03:05.509 --> 00:03:09.551
Pursued by larger fish, they make for shallow waters near the shore.

56
00:03:10.371 --> 00:03:11.792
And that's where we'll catch them."

57
00:03:12.712 --> 00:03:14.453
We soon had a fishery in operation.

58
00:03:15.173 --> 00:03:18.835
Jack and Fritz stood in the water with baskets and scooped up the fish.

59
00:03:19.875 --> 00:03:24.406
After cleaning the fish, Ernest and I preserved them in barrels with layers of salt.

60
00:03:25.207 --> 00:03:29.750
When the barrels were full, we rolled them away to the cool storage room at the back of

61
00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:30.311
our cave.

62
00:03:31.291 --> 00:03:35.935
Now we had not only a place to live during the rainy season, but also some more food.

63
00:03:37.336 --> 00:03:39.397
And our good fortune did not end with the herring.

64
00:03:40.598 --> 00:03:43.840
About a month later, Jack spied another shoal of fish.

65
00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:49.684
I ran down to the shore with him and realized that these big fish were sturgeon.

66
00:03:49.704 --> 00:03:51.525
Jack was delighted with his discovery.

67
00:03:51.545 --> 00:03:53.745
"These are proper fish!"

68
00:03:54.065 --> 00:03:54.705
he exclaimed.

69
00:03:54.725 --> 00:03:57.026
"Not like those puny herring.

70
00:03:57.426 --> 00:03:59.807
How do you preserve these fish—salted or smoked?"

71
00:04:00.527 --> 00:04:01.887
"Not so fast," I replied.

72
00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:05.668
"Tell me how you're going to catch them, and I'll tell you how to preserve them."

73
00:04:06.609 --> 00:04:09.089
"Oh, I'll catch them soon enough," he replied.

74
00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:13.731
He darted off to Rockburg and returned with a bow and arrow and a ball of string.

75
00:04:14.611 --> 00:04:18.072
After tying the string to the arrow, he advanced to the water's edge.

76
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.442
His arrow flew from the bow, and to my surprise struck one of the largest fish.

77
00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:25.863
"Help! Father, help!"

78
00:04:26.123 --> 00:04:28.524
cried Jack as the fish darted away with the arrow.

79
00:04:28.544 --> 00:04:31.485
"Help! Or he will pull me into the water!"

80
00:04:32.326 --> 00:04:35.207
I ran to his side and together we struggled with the huge fish.

81
00:04:36.027 --> 00:04:38.468
As soon as we landed it, Fritz and Ernest appeared.

82
00:04:39.268 --> 00:04:41.989
Eager to participate, they rushed off for fishing equipment.

83
00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:48.132
They returned and were soon in the water, Fritz with a harpoon and Ernest with a fishing rod.

84
00:04:49.048 --> 00:04:52.432
After some time Fritz managed to harpoon a gigantic sturgeon.

85
00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:55.696
But he couldn't land it even with our help.

86
00:04:56.818 --> 00:04:58.119
"I'd hate to lose this fish."

87
00:04:58.820 --> 00:04:59.361
He panted.

88
00:04:59.381 --> 00:05:01.843
"It must be eight feet long."
